    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d249">

                <pb facs="0281" n="191" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg191"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1989</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Irkutsk</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d249-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Irkutsk</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1918-06-10T15:22:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1918-06-06T14:00:00+08:26"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June
                            10, 1918, 3.22 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Following from [Emerson and Slaughter,] Suslovo near Mariinsk: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>On return Mariinsk June 2<anchor n="2" xml:id="d249-f2"/> found Czechs
                            and Russians reenforcing, strongly entrenched, 3,500 Czechs, 2,500
                            Russians ready fight. We attempted mediate, secured armistice until June
                            10. Now going Vladivostok with Russian and Czech representatives secure
                            final settlement. Russians will never agree permit Czechs proceed fully
                            armed; Czechs want to learn [<hi rendition="#italic">sic</hi>], rear
                            trains safe to [omission]. White Guards Novo Nikolaevsk, Tomsk,
                            Mariinsk, taken advantage <pb facs="0282" n="192" xml:id="pg192"/>
                            situation to arm and take control places named. Czechs refuse permit
                            Russians resume authority places prior their departure. Details by
                            Thomas leaving to-day.</p></quote></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harris</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d249-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d249-f1-content">
                <p>Sent via the Consulates at Vladivostok and Kobe.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d249-f2" n="2" xml:id="d249-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram of June 15, 2 p.m., from the Consul at Vladivostok, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg212">p. 212</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
